#!/usr/sbin/nft -f
# Install to /etc/stratoscan/filter.nft (loaded by stratoscan-filter.service).
#
# Nobody on the internet opens a connection to the radar over IPv6 (security
# review 2026-11-03, SR-10; IPv6-only since 2026-11-15).
#
# The radar's page (80, and 443/4443 for the app's setup) and SSH (22) listen
# on every address the radar has, and on a network with IPv6 one of those is
# a global address. Whether the internet can reach it there is up to the home
# router, and in someone else's house the router is unknown.
#
# IPv4 IS NOT FILTERED AT ALL. The first rule accepts every IPv4 packet, from
# any address, to any port, exactly as with no filter. A home router shares
# one IPv4 address between everything behind it (NAT), so nothing outside
# reaches the radar over IPv4 unless the owner forwards a port to it. And an
# earlier version of this file, which let IPv4 in only from the private
# ranges, would have shut an owner out of a radar on a home network numbered
# outside them: SSH from that network is the one way into a radar with no
# keyboard.
#
# IPv6. Always let in:
#   - answers to what the radar itself asked for (established, related);
#   - the radar itself (loopback), which is also how its screen reaches it;
#   - anything arriving on the tailnet's interface (tailscale0): the
#     tailnet's own policy decides there;
#   - all ICMPv6: ping, path MTU, and neighbour discovery and router
#     advertisements, without which the radar has no IPv6 at all;
#   - anything sent from a link-local (fe80::/10) or unique-local (fc00::/7)
#     address: the house. Neither is routed on the internet;
#   - the answers of the home router's DHCPv6 (to port 546, at the radar's
#     link-local address);
#   - Bonjour (mDNS) sent to its multicast group (ff02::fb), which never
#     crosses a router.
# Every other new inbound IPv6 connection is refused: TCP with a reset, the
# same answer a closed port gives, so a phone or laptop that tried the
# radar's global IPv6 address first moves straight on to its IPv4 one instead
# of waiting out a timeout; the rest is dropped. That includes a phone or
# laptop in the house when it uses its own global IPv6 address: it gets the
# reset and carries on over IPv4.
#
# Tailscale's own UDP port is not opened to the internet over IPv6. Two
# tailnet devices still reach each other: the radar's own packets to the
# other device make the answers "established", and failing that the tailnet
# uses IPv4 or its relays.
#
# What this does not touch: anything the radar sends, forwarding and NAT (the
# setup hotspot's sharing is NetworkManager's), the 443 redirect
# (stratoscan-https.nft), a managed unit's SSH rule (stratoscan-managed.nft)
# and Tailscale's own tables. A packet must pass every table, so those rules
# and these add up; none replaces another.
#
# It replaces only its own table, never the whole ruleset. (The empty
# declaration first lets the delete succeed when the table is not there yet.)
# The chain's policy is accept and the refusal is the last two rules. nft
# loads a file whole or not at all: if any line here is refused, nothing
# changes and the radar is reachable as it was without it.
#
# AFTER ANY EDIT this file must be checked again by nft itself, on a radar,
# in a private network namespace where it can change nothing:
#     unshare -rn nft -c -f stratoscan-filter.nft
# The version before this one was checked that way, and loaded there, on
# 2026-11-15. This version uses the same constructs and one more, the first
# rule's `meta nfproto ipv4`, and has not been checked that way yet. The
# image build runs the same check and fails on an error (image/build.sh), and
# the installer asks nft before it switches the filter on.
#
# Stopping stratoscan-filter.service takes the table away again. Until the
# service is enabled, so does a power cycle.
table inet stratoscan_filter
delete table inet stratoscan_filter
table inet stratoscan_filter {
    set house6 {
        type ipv6_addr
        flags interval
        elements = { fe80::/10, fc00::/7 }
    }
    chain input {
        type filter hook input priority 10; policy accept;
        meta nfproto ipv4 accept
        ct state established,related accept
        iifname "lo" accept
        iifname "tailscale0" accept
        meta l4proto ipv6-icmp accept
        ip6 saddr @house6 accept
        ip6 daddr fe80::/10 udp dport 546 accept
        ip6 daddr ff02::fb udp dport 5353 accept
        meta l4proto tcp counter reject with tcp reset
        counter drop
    }
}
